(4127092, 588375, 19982, '<td valign="top">Love the colors ... you might want to turn the sharpening down, especially around the tree.  What I usually do is sharpen the whole picture, then use the History Brush to undo the sharpening around the edges that become too sharp.</td>', 'Love the colors ... you might want to turn the sharpening down, especially around the tree.  What I usually do is sharpen the whole picture, then use the History Brush to undo the sharpening around the edges that become too sharp.', '2007-10-01 01:31:58.000000', NULL, 1),
(4127093, 591620, 43667, '<td valign="top">Is this your child?!!!</td>', 'Is this your child?!!!', '2007-10-01 01:32:09.000000', NULL, 1),
(4127119, 593703, 19982, '<td valign="top">The blue sky is just way too fake.  First, given the lack of color in the mountains, you''d expect the sky to similarly lack color, an instead it''s an intense blue (a color not normally found in the sky).  Second, the color is too uniform.  The sky is always a gradient, it is never a single color.  This looks like the sky was simply filled in with a single color.<br/> <br/>Next time, try something like this:<br/>1) Select the sky using the same method you used here<br/>2) Now duplicate the sky on another layer and set that layer''s blend mode to multiply<br/>3) Adjust the opacity of the new layer down to where it looks normal<br/> <br/>The above will take the existing colors of the sky and simply intensify them by using the multiply blend mode.<br/> <br/>If the sky is completely washed out ... then you could try filling it (like you did here), but instead use a gradient.  Pick two colors of blue (one lighter than the other) as your foreground and background colors.  Then use the gradient tool to apply the color to your selected area.  I would also do this on another layer so that you could then adjust the opacity down to where it looks good.</td>', 'The blue sky is just way too fake.  First, given the lack of color in the mountains, you''d expect the sky to similarly lack color, an instead it''s an intense blue (a color not normally found in the sky).  Second, the color is too uniform.  The sky is always a gradient, it is never a single color.  This looks like the sky was simply filled in with a single color. Next time, try something like this:1) Select the sky using the same method you used here2) Now duplicate the sky on another layer and set that layer''s blend mode to multiply3) Adjust the opacity of the new layer down to where it looks normal The above will take the existing colors of the sky and simply intensify them by using the multiply blend mode. If the sky is completely washed out ... then you could try filling it (like you did here), but instead use a gradient.  Pick two colors of blue (one lighter than the other) as your foreground and background colors.  Then use the gradient tool to apply the color to your selected area.  I would also do this on another layer so that you could then adjust the opacity down to where it looks good.', '2007-10-01 01:37:30.000000', NULL, 1),
